      "explanation": "The tests are c8 (bad), c4 (bad), c2 (good) and c3 (bad). Each answer keeps only the half that can still hold the first bad commit, so a bug near the start also takes 4 tests.",
      "concept_ids": [
        "git-bisect"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "exit-125",
      "kind": "predict",
      "at": {
        "scene": "run"
      },
      "prompt": "With git bisect run, your test script exits with code 125 on one commit. What does Git do with that commit?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "text": "It skips the commit, because it cannot be tested",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "text": "It marks the commit bad",
          "misconception": "Thinks every nonzero exit code means bad"
        },
        {
          "text": "It aborts the whole bisect",
          "misconception": "Thinks 125 is outside the range bisect run understands"
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "The git-bisect manual reserves exit code 125 for \"cannot be tested\": Git skips that revision, as with git bisect skip. Codes 1 to 127 except 125 mean bad, 0 means good.",
      "concept_ids": [
        "git-bisect"
      ],
      "revisit": "run"
    },
    {
      "id": "exit-255",
      "kind": "verify",
      "at": {
        "tab": "practice"
      },
      "prompt": "Your test script ends with exit(-1), so the shell sees exit code 255. What does git bisect run do?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "text": "It aborts the bisect",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "text": "It marks the commit bad",
          "misconception": "Thinks any failure code counts as bad, but only 1 to 127 (not 125) do"
        },
        {
          "text": "It marks the commit good",
          "misconception": "Thinks a negative exit code reads as zero"
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "Only 0 (good), 1 to 127 except 125 (bad) and 125 (skip) have a meaning. Any other code, such as 255, aborts the bisect process.",
      "concept_ids": [
        "git-bisect"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "skip-adjacent",
      "kind": "verify",
      "at": {
        "tab": "practice"
      },
      "prompt": "In the skip preset, c11 is good, c12 was skipped and c13 is bad. Why can Git not name one first bad commit?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "text": "The skipped commit is next to the one you look for, so either c12 or c13 could be first",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Skip deletes c12 from the history",
          "misconception": "Thinks git bisect skip changes the commits, but it only stops Git from testing one"
        },
        {
          "text": "Git needs one more test of c13",
          "misconception": "Thinks a commit already marked bad must be tested again"
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "The manual warns: if you skip a commit next to the one you are looking for, Git cannot tell which of those commits was the first bad one. Without a result for c12, both c12 and c13 remain possible.",
      "concept_ids": [
        "git-bisect"
      ]
